    "result_text": "The full test suite is green \u2014 all 29 tests pass, including the new `Cp18Tests` city-capacity check.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the rule: **creating an owner is rejected with 409 when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners.**\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, I added a new guard step to the `POST /api/owners` pipeline, modeled on the existing `EnsureUniqueHousehold` / `EnsureUniqueTelephone` steps:\n\n1. **`EnsureCityCapacity`** (`.../function/owner/EnsureCityCapacity.java`) \u2014 counts existing owners whose city matches the request's city (case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed, consistent with the other rules) and throws a checked `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is \u2265 50.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException`** (`.../escalation/`) \u2014 checked exception so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`** (`.../escalation/`) \u2014 responds `409 Conflict` with a `ProblemDetail` body, matching the existing duplicate-household/telephone handlers.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** (`officefloor/escalation/...CityAtCapacityException.yml`) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `ensureCityCapacity` step (governed by `transaction`) between `ensureUniqueHousehold` and `build`.\n\nVerified: `Cp18Tests` (fill a city to 50, 51st rejected as 409) passes, and no existing test regressed.",
